#basePath TestChapter

:: Preload
@function    ChapterPreload

$call SetBackground("bg/Room.png")

// #region Bedroom 

:: Start
@trigger    ChapterStart
@cutscene

{@}     Reality arrives swiftly and disorientingly.

{@Player}   Welp, another one for my therapist.

{@}     On your desk, you spot several notes you've taken.

$call GiveEvidenceCard("Evidence-Lamprey-Discovered")

// #endregion 

// #region Docks
 
$goto .TravelDocks

:: TravelDocks

{@}     Your remember hearing about Sea Lampreys. 
        The Anglers by the docks were making whispers of them. 
        
$choice .Docks.A; Guess I should go ask those anglers. 

:: .Docks.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the docks.

$goto .AnglersDocks

:: AnglersDocks

{@}     As you approach the docks, you see two Anglers. 

{@Angler1}      
        Never thought we'd see the day. 
{@Angler2}      
        I've been tellin' ya, they gettin' bolder. 

{@}     
        You decide to say hello. 
{@Player}   
        Hi there! 

{@}    
        They don't seem to notice you. 

{@Angler1}     
        Take a closer look. 

{@}     He holds the fish up to you. 
        You notice that the bite marks are nothing familiar to the region.
        Infact, they're uniquely circular.  
        You take a picture of it on your phone. 

{@Angler2} 
        They have been on many of the fish we catch. Whitefish and Walleye. 

{@Angler1} 
        And Lamprey feed on them. 
        
$call GiveEvidenceCard("Evidence-Lake-Walleye")

$call GiveEvidenceCard("Evidence-Lake-Trout")

{@Angler1} 
        A good chunk of those fish wind up dead. 

{@Angler2}
        Dead fish means rotten fish. 

{@Angler1} 
        Rotten fish means you can't sell the fish. 

{@Player} 
        Not only are you guys out of your jobs, but the entire town's economy is at threat.

{@}     The anglers nod their heads in unison. 
        You think for a moment, understanding the gravity of the situation.

{@Player} 
        What can I do to help? 

{@Angler1}   
        Talking to someone who knows more about Sea Lamprey's may be a good idea. 
        Maybe going to the <i>Museum<i> or even the <i>University Researh Lab<i>. 

{@Angler2} 
        Going to the <i>Town Hall<i> may be a good idea as well. 

{@} 
        You think to yourself, where would be the best next place to go. 

$choice .Nature.Museum.A; Nature Museum
$choice .University.Research.Lab.A; The Research Lab
$choice .Town.Hall.A; Town Hall
$choose branch

:: Nature.Museum

{@Player} The Museum seems the best place to go. 

{@Angler1} Good choice, they may provide more info on what's going on. 

$goto .Nature.Museum.Location

:: University.Research.Lab.A

{@Player} The University Research Lab. I remember hearing they were studying lampreys. 

{@Angler2} Aye, they might put a stop to this. 

$goto .University.Research.Lab.Location


:: Town.Hall.A

{@Player} I can always bring it to Town Hall. 

{@Angler1} Ah. Politics. Have fun convincing them to do anything. 

$goto .Town.Hall.Location.A

//endregion 

// #region Museum 

:: Nature.Museum.Location.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the Museum.
        You see the curator entranced in a book. It's titled <i>Sea Lamprey<i> 
        She speaks to you, still reading her book. 

{@Curator}
        Welcome to the Nature Museum. Tours on the right. Studies on the left. Have fun. 

{@Player} 
        Actually, I was wondering if I could learn more about Sea Lamprey. 

{@}     The Curator's eyes glance up at you for the first time. They light up. 

{@Curator} 
        You're interested in Sea Lamprey? 

{@Player} 
        Uh... yes-- 

{@} 
        The curator shoots up, digs under their desk and lays out several documents and books. 
        All of the books are related to Sea Lamprey. 
        The Curator has an eager grin on their face. 

{@Curator} 
        What do you want to know? 

$choice .Lamprey.Bites.A; Could you confirm that these bites are Sea Lamprey? 

:: Lamprey.Bites.A

{@}
        You show the curator the picture of the bite on your phone. 

{@Curator}
        Yes. Sea Lamprey are known for their circular bites. This came from the docks? 

{@}     You nod your head. 

{@Curator} 
        This would make sense, given my research. 

{@}     
        That confirms the anglers concerns. 

{@Curator}
        What else did you want to learn about Sea Lamprey? 

$choice .Lamprey.Stop; How do we put a stop to the Sea Lamprey? 
$choice .Lamprey.Origin.A; Where are they coming from?  
$choice .Lamprey.Invasive.A; Is there any vital info I should know about Sea Lamprey? 
$choose branch 

:: Lamprey.Origin.A

{@Curator} 
        Sea Lamprey originated from the canals that lead to the Atalantic. 
        They were introduced to the region when shipping routes were made to bypass Niagra Falls.

$choice .Lamprey.Invasive.B; What else can you tell me about Sea Lamprey? 
$choice .Lamprey.Stop; How do we stop them? 
$choose branch

:: Lamprey.Origin.B

{@Curator}     
        They came from the canals that lead to the Atlantic. 
        They only became a problem when those shipping canals were created. 

$choice .Lamprey.Stop; How do we stop them? 

:: Lamprey.Invasive.A

{@Curator}
        Sea Lamprey are parasitic, and they have a tendency to feed on lake fish. 
        They're invasive, but they aren't the <i>only<i> species of lamprey. 
        There are native lamprey species, so targeting Sea Lamprey alone without effecting other Lamprey species can be tricky. 

$choice .Lamprey.Origin.B; Where did they come from? 
$choice .Lamprey.Stop; How do we stop them? 
$choose branch

:: Lamprey.Invasive.B 

{@Curator} 
        They're parasitic, they suck on the blood of lake fish. 
        While invasive, Lamprey's in general are natural to the region. 
        It's only sea lamprey that prove to be a problem. 

$choice .Lamprey.Stop; How do we stop them? 

:: Lamprey.Stop 

{@Curator} 
        Well, there are several ways to stop Sea Lamprey. 
        You can introduce sterile male lampreys into the population. The Fish Hatchery specializes in that.
        There's lampricide, which one of the Field Stations' are working on. 
        There's barriers, both physical and electric. The Corps of Engineers can make those.
        Regardless, all of these methods would need to be approved by the Town Hall, and you can't use all of them. 

$choice .Fish.Hacthery.A; I should learn more about introducing sterile lampreys. 
$choice .Field.Station.Trib.A; Lampricide sounds quite effective. 
$choice .Corps.Engineers.A; Nothing beats a barrier. 
$choose branch 

:: Fish.Hatchery.A 
{@Curator} 
        Head towards the Fish Hatchery. 

$goto .Fish.Hacthery.Location.A 

:: Field.Station.Trib.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the Field Station. 

$goto .Field.Station.Trib.Location.A

:: Corps.Engineers.A 

{@} 
        You make your way to the Army Corps of Engineers Office. 


//endregion 

// #region University Lab

:: University.Research.Lab.Location

{@} 
        You make your way to the University Research Lab.
        A researcher clacks away at his computer. 

{@Researcher}
        Hi there! How can I help you? 

{@Player} 
        I was hoping to get some more information regarding Sea Lamprey. 

{@Researcher}
        You're in the right place! Anything in particular? 

$choice .Lamprey.Bites.B; Can you confirm that these are Sea Lamprey bites. 
$choice .Lamprey.Pheremones; How might you approach stopping Sea Lamprey? 
$choose branch 

{@Player} Pheremones seems like a good way to go about this. 

{@Researcher} 
They're effective. If you want to learn more go to the DNR office. 
However, they're not the only method.
You can always introduce sterile Sea Lamprey into the population, which the Fish Hatchery knows a lot about! 
Or use Lampricide, something that the researchers at the Field Station have been working on. 

$choice .DNR.Office.A; The DNR Office 
$choice .Field.Station.Trib.B; The Field Station
$choice .Fish.Hatchery.B; The Fish Hacthery 
$choose branch 

:: Lamprey.Bites.B

{@}     
        You show the Researcher the photo you took.

{@Researcher} 
        Yes... very concerning. Seems like the Sea Lamprey in our region are getting worse. 

$choice .Lamprey.Phermones; How might you get rid of them?

:: Lamprey.Pheremones

{@Researcher} 
        Well, we're studying the use phermones as a lure. 
        Sea Lamprey are sensitive to all sorts of smells, especially their own pheremones to mate. 
        So, using their phermones to attract them make them easy to trap. 
        Once trapped, we remove the females from the trap, to reduce the population. 

:: DNR.Office.A 

{@} 
        You make your way to the DNR Office. 

$goto .DNR.Office.Location.A

:: Field.Station.Trib.B

{@}
        You make your way to the Field Station

$goto .Field.Station.Trib.Location.B

:: .Fish.Hatchery.B

{@}    
        You make your way to the Fish Hacthery

$goto .Fish.Hacthery.Location.B

// endregion 

// #region Town Hall (A)

:: Town.Hall.Location.A 

{@}     You walk towards the Town Hall. 
        You enter the building and it just so happens the mayor is standing in the lobby. 
        How convenient. 

{@Player}      
        Madam Mayor!

{@Mayor} 
        Hi! Do... I know you? 

{@Player} 
        I'm investigating the Sea Lamprey in town. Mayor, it's serious. We need to do something. 

{@Mayor} 
        I see, well, I can't just do anything based on hearsay. You got anything to back up these claims? 

$choice .Town.Hall.B; Uh... well, I came here first. 

:: Town.Hall.B

{@Mayor}
        Well, that's not the worst idea. you'll have to convince me and the council. 
        There needs a plan. Which means you need more info and more people on your side. 
        And money. 
        I'd suggest going to the Nature Museum or the University Research Lab.
 
$choice .Nature.Museum.B; Nature Museum
$choice .University.Research.Lab.B; The Research Lab
$choose branch 

:: Nature.Museum.B

{@} You make your way to the Nature Museum 

$goto .Nature.Museum.Location 

:: University.Research.Lab.B

{@} You make your way to the University Research Lab

$goto .University.Research.Lab.Location